\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Cell-incidence walks retain signs and transports}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{For a finite free fiber with the
\vocabk{invariant cochain pairing}{fdip-00SR}, the power traces of
#{\extD_U} and #{\cov_U} are sums over closed walks among the
labeled cochain cells. Each walk contributes the fiber trace of
the ordered product of its signed incidence maps. For #{\cov_U} each lowering
step has an additional minus sign. For #{r\ge 0},
##{\begin{aligned}
\trace(\cov_U^{2r})&=(-1)^r\trace(\extD_U^{2r}),\\
\trace(\cov_U^{2r+1})&=\trace(\extD_U^{2r+1})=0.
\end{aligned}}
In rank one, the determinants of #{m\bbI+\extD_U} and
#{m\bbI+\cov_U} are signed sums over cycle covers with these
same step weights, allowing short cycles and mass fixed points.}

\proof{
\p{Choose a basis of the free fiber and order the cells.
For a cell label #{(x,I)} and #{i\notin I}, put
#{\epsilon(i,I)=(-1)^{|\{j\in I:j<i\}|}}.
The raising block contributing to the output label
#{(x,I\cup\{i\})} has two occurrences:
##{\begin{array}{c|c}
 \text{input label}&\text{fiber map}\\\hline
 (x+\varepsilon_i,I)&\epsilon(i,I)\rho(U_i(x))^{-1}\\
 (x,I)&-\epsilon(i,I)\bbI_F.
\end{array}}
Reversing each occurrence gives its adjoint for the lowering
part of #{\extD_U}, by \ref{fdip-00SS}. For #{\cov_U} the
reversed maps are the negatives of those adjoints. Even when
periodic endpoints coincide, the two labeled occurrences remain
separate summands.}

\p{Matrix multiplication selects a sequence of such
occurrences with matching cell labels. Its contribution is
the ordered product of the fiber maps, including all written
signs. A diagonal block closes the walk; its fiber trace then
contributes to the full trace. Each raising step increases the
cochain degree by one and each lowering step decreases it by one.
A closed walk has equally many of each. Thus it has even length
#{2r}, and precisely #{r} factors change sign for #{\cov_U}.
A plaquette transport alone omits the incidence signs and the
cochain degrees visited by the walk.}

\p{For rank-one fibers, the determinant is the signed sum over
permutations of the cell labels. Decomposing each permutation
into cycles gives the cycle cover. Diagonal choices use the
mass, and two-cycles can use a raising occurrence and a
lowering occurrence. The sign of the permutation is the same
for both determinants. In the product of its weights, each
chosen lowering occurrence for #{\cov_U} contributes the extra
minus sign, while every mass fixed point contributes #{m}.}
}
